 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The per-segment computation is dominated by SIMD arithmetic operations (multiplication, addition, subtraction, minimum) with latencies of 4-6 cycles [S4, unofficial]. The pre-computation step is time-consuming but done once per transfer function change [S5].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The 1D table approach uses 4 Kbytes (256 entries × 16 bytes), which fits in the L1 cache of typical CPUs, resulting in a high hit ratio and avoiding the cache misses associated with larger 2D tables (512 Kbytes) [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On a Pentium-4 2GHz, pre-integrated rendering (d=0.45) achieved 0.158s per frame (6.33 fps) compared to 0.295s for standard classification with empty-space skipping [S4, unofficial]. A 200 MHz CPU with an nVidia GeForce3 achieved 4 fps for a 256x256 viewport [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Reduces aliasing artifacts caused by high frequencies in non-linear transfer functions [S4, unofficial][S5]. Allows for larger ray step sizes (coarser sampling) while maintaining image quality, effectively increasing the Nyquist frequency for the scalar field reconstruction independent of the transfer function [S1, unofficial][S5].
-*   **Limitations:** Assumes linear scalar variation between samples, which can cause artifacts in regions of high curvature [S6, unofficial]. Not directly applicable to high-dimensional transfer functions without significant memory overhead [S8, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Suitable for interactive volume rendering of regular grid data with non-linear transfer functions, especially on hardware with limited memory bandwidth or cache [S4, S7, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When the scalar field varies highly non-linearly within segments and adaptive sampling is not used [S6, unofficial], or when using high-dimensional transfer functions [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The per-raypoint computation is dominated by SIMD arithmetic operations (MULPS, ADDPS, SUBPS, MINPS, RCPPS) with latencies of 4-6 cycles [S4, unofficial]. The pre-computation of the lookup table is time-consuming, especially for high-dimensional transfer functions [S5].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The 1D table implementation uses 4 Kbytes (256 entries $\times$ 4 floats $\times$ 4 bytes), which fits in the L1 cache, avoiding cache misses associated with larger 2D tables (e.g., 512 Kbytes) [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On a Pentium-4 2GHz, pre-integrated rendering (d=0.45, simplified alpha) took 0.158s for a 256x256 viewport, compared to 0.295s for standard classification with empty-space skipping [S4, unofficial]. Pre-integration increases speed by a factor of 10 for the linear model compared to baseline [S3, abstract only, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Reduces aliasing artifacts caused by high frequencies in non-linear transfer functions [S4, unofficial][S5]. Allows for larger ray step sizes (coarser sampling) while maintaining image quality [S4, unofficial]. Decouples sampling from transfer function evaluation [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Assumes linear scalar progression between samples, which can cause artifacts [S6, unofficial]. Not directly applicable to high-dimensional transfer functions without restrictions [S8, unofficial]. The simplified alpha approximation may reduce contrast [S4, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Suitable for interactive volume rendering on CPUs or GPUs where transfer functions are non-linear and high-frequency, and where memory bandwidth is a bottleneck [S4, S7, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When the scalar field varies highly non-linearly within segments and adaptive sampling is not used [S6, unofficial]. When high-dimensional transfer functions are required without simplification [S8, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Pre-integrated Volume Rendering was introduced by Engel et al. in 2001 [Engel01] as an improvement over basic texture mapping to overcome high Nyquist frequencies from non-linear transfer functions [S1, unofficial]. It was later combined with volumetric clipping and advanced lighting effects by Röttger et al. in 2003 [Röttger03]. The method separates the DVR integral into two parts: one for the continuous scalar value and one for the transfer function parameters (colors and extinction coefficient) [S5]. It is implemented in software systems like UltraVis [S4, unofficial] and has been adapted for GPU-based raycasting [S5].
+Pre-integrated volume rendering was introduced by Engel et al. in 2001 [S1, S7, unofficial]. It was developed to overcome the problem of high Nyquist frequencies resulting from non-linear transfer functions in texture-based volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. The method splits the numerical integration into two parts: one for the continuous scalar field and one for the transfer function [S1, unofficial]. It was later combined with volumetric clipping and advanced lighting effects by Röttger et al. [S1, unofficial]. The technique has been implemented in software systems like UltraVis [S4, unofficial] and adapted for unstructured grids [S2, unofficial].
 
